== AS-IS

+ Write a `[[tasks.rules]]` entry with `name`, `assert` and `message`, and no `severity`.
+ File a task that breaks it with `mt add`, filling the template's `summary` and `acceptance` slots.
+ 🔴 `mt add` prints a warning and writes the task anyway:

```
$ mt add "Try the refusal." --set kind=feat --set priority=5 --set assignee=ada \
    --set summary="Breaks the rule." --set acceptance="It is refused."
warning: <new task from template 'task'>:3: rules.no-full-stop-in-titles: drop the full stop at the end of the title
wrote tasks/try-the-refusal.typ (05t)
```

== Environment

- `mt` built from `main` at `978533e3`, on Linux.

== TO-BE

- 🟢 A `[[tasks.rules]]` or `[[commits.rules]]` entry with no `severity` refuses the write, like one that says `error`.
- 🟢 A project with an advisory rule writes `severity = "warn"`, and the rule reports without failing the command.
- 🟢 The schema and `www/content/rules.typ` both say the default is `error`. A project reads the level before it writes a rule.

```
$ mt add "Try the refusal." --set kind=feat --set priority=5 --set assignee=ada \
    --set summary="Breaks the rule." --set acceptance="It is refused."
error: <new task from template 'task'>:3: rules.no-full-stop-in-titles: drop the full stop at the end of the title
```

== Changes

- `crates/mindtape/src/validate/severity.rs`: `Default` returns `Error`, and the doc comment says so.
- `crates/mindtape/src/rules/mod.rs`: the `RuleSpec.severity` doc says the default is `error`.
- `crates/mindtape/src/config/commits.rs`: the `CommitRuleSpec.severity` doc says the same.
- `crates/mindtape/src/rules/tests.rs`, `crates/mindtape/src/config/commits_tests.rs`: a rule with no `severity` reports at `error`.
- `examples/rules/.mindtape/config.toml`: `no-full-stop-in-titles` says `severity = "warn"`, so the fixture still shows a warning.
- `www/content/rules.typ`: the default is `error`, and a project writes `warn` to make a rule advisory.
- `schema/config.schema.json`: regenerated by `mt schema --write`.
- `CHANGELOG.md`: an entry under `[Unreleased]` `Changed`.

== Out of scope

- The body rule severity. `[[tasks.body.headers.rules]]` and `[[tasks.body.content.rules]]` already report at `error`.
- A migration for a project that relied on the old default. The release notes state the change, and the fix is one line per rule.

== Dogfood

This repo writes `severity = "error"` on every rule it declares, so nothing changes here. The one rule that relied on the default is the `examples/rules` fixture, which gains an explicit `warn`.
